/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1031 "Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1033 178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050519 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "50225243-6d83-5aa6-a6f4-fff184fce3db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104680 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f61b895f-2b53-547b-9ce6-170508475559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104681 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104681 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104681 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b9b6ece2-c175-5a62-a4c8-d3a7bc1bcb02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104682 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104682 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104682 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bac5f487-8251-5e3f-a25d-1940879a8ba1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104683 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104683 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104683 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d191d7ec-f08f-53da-9bdf-ef6d460a8ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104684 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104684 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104684 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc1687fe-5277-552d-af56-af2b924dbf53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104685 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104685 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186104685 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5333d1c5-bab1-5a7b-8948-71d6f629ad61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105557 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105557 #edb/ref 1071 "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $4,$ a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $P$ on the plane.\n\n \n\n![](images/e6/e6ede881702a8713cf9c878f9a9d7fbdd0bdcb14bcab500d3cf256cc9cc84463.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $OP \\leq 4$\n\nII. $OC = 4$\n\nIII. $OP > 4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b2211b8-cc3f-5dd6-9dc0-1dc1a7f69513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105557 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nOC = 4.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the point $P$ lies strictly outside the circle. So, the distance between $P$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOP > OC = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105558 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105558 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the diagram below. What is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ if $OQ=3?$\n\n![](images/4e/4e32346cc949b0f17ed219e44d4600abd23a0839a3855fcf76ff4060f322698c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e6ebec8-4c08-5bc9-8a8c-4c6cedb2bd8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105558 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $Q$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r =OQ=3.$ \n\nTo find $AB,$ we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB = AO + OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overline{AO}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $3,$ we have\n\n$$\nAO=3, \\qquad OB=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB = AO+OB = 3 + 3= 6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105559 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105559 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/606c8bb2352215fe5c34acb78742034df016b8f41c7a6194b700e861f4f35596.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord of the circle\n\nII. $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius of the circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb990fba-991a-58c8-a404-54353183903c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105559 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. Therefore,  $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle and $\\overline{BC}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement III is true. The segment $\\overline{OC}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About the Distance to a Circle's Center" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105693 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "82c825b9-fc36-510c-982e-e4596ab2ce24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105694 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8fa963eb-82a7-56cb-a8b8-f9291adec2d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105695 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Line Segments Associated with a Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105695 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105695 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2401d489-0970-54a8-a70d-9e8613458f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1045 "Circles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106022 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "53739364-ab76-5f6a-9987-a1e5894cdc33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186104680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106422 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3f772ad4b54217a479b63a6919a915af7bd8f44013687dae29712bf09624c68b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a radius of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a chord of the circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36da278f-fa98-5fed-9b0f-aa9974a3b0ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106422 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle and $\\overset{―}{AB}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement II is false since both endpoints of $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a chord but not a radius.\n\n- Statement III is false since the endpoint $O$ does not lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a radius but not a chord.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106548 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106548 #edb/ref 1061 10836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9812b925-7461-5557-92e8-1ff7d3367b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106549 #edb/ref 1061 10837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16717d1c-e42a-524b-915d-835d1e83173b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106550 #edb/ref 1061 10838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "432ff13e-3f9e-5ca8-8549-971f7f9795b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106551 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106551 #edb/ref 1061 10839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106551 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7fb8225e-7462-53e8-804b-4a3bdd2a3605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106551 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106552 #edb/ref 1061 10840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106552 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ea7aa1c-cdcf-5530-838a-3d3d9f236858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106552 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $O$ be a point on the plane. Let's consider all the points on the plane whose distance to $O$ is equal to $5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0fc787b4469b0322fa46fa072d58ae163e24dea284f3e5507306e9b30dfbf3.png)\n\nThe collection of all such points defines a geometrical shape which is called a **circle**. \n\n- The point $O$ is called the **center** of the circle.\n\n- The distance from $O$ to any point on the circle is called the **radius**.\n\nIn our diagram above, the radius is $r={\\color{black}5\\,\\textrm{cm}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1065 2417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a53c8e3d-2c5a-558c-912f-f2e051ef7b41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1064 "There are three important line segments associated with a circle:\n\n- A **radius** is any line segment that connects a point on the circle with the center (left-hand diagram).\n\n- A **chord** is any line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle (middle diagram).\n\n- A **diameter** is any chord that passes through the center of the circle (right-hand diagram). The diameter is the longest chord and its measure is twice the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nd = 2r.\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f12a9db3210054e64397e26c87fd57ce4e55ff5dc9a2ed0d3b0ce3a8f9c2e65.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1065 2418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bcbdf407-5fe9-57d7-9359-167f8fbf21b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1067 "Chords and Diameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496687 #edb/ref 1080 "7.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496687 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496687 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ef873a9-5bec-5e2b-b660-6cbad6be6522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496760 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f1dce06-b6df-5198-871d-5f7df6305d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496761 #edb/ref 1080 "OB=15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f567857-fdbe-5e15-8e1d-74e3283869bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218649676