ref 17592186045417] [17592186497279 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497279 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "85a6d838-1d50-5cf7-85ea-9d3d39581030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497313 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4f08898-f474-54e8-8d99-f01d18e59d63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497339 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/44e48aadd601b6b9c66358bac848748f6a752d2144df500dfb0dfcf6aec105da.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $17,$ a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $Q$ on the plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $OQ<17$\n\nII. $OQ>17$\n\nIII. $OC>17$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d4d18d1-b26f-52d1-a7f8-17af1daba334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497339 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nOC=17\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the point $Q$ lies outside the circle. So, the distance between $Q$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOQ>OC=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true, while statements I and III are false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497367 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497367 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a circle with center $O$ and radius $r=14,$ and the point $B$ that lies in the same plane as the circle. Which of the following conditions might be true if it's known that the point $B$ lies outside the circle?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6da0fd72-e546-5bf3-b264-a420d5d83b72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497367 #edb/ref 1074 "We will sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/60/60056e6bc44989185f3707ba013bb81d440b8a7060e5c754608e8c5977db687e.png)\n\nSince $B$ lies outside the circle, the distance between $B$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\nOB>r=14.\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only $OB=15$ satisfies the above inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497369 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/29/2981b6af8c364d33211155585768059d7fdde44eb99717019ab6d668ff09f6b3.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ if $OC=16?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7240a23b-2aa5-51b9-83e2-173faee26862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497369 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r=OC=16.$ \n\nTo find $AB,$ we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB=AO+OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $16,$ we have \n\n$$\nAO=16,\\,OB=16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB=AO+OB=16+16=32.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497398 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497398 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497398 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497398 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8cf46e7f0e0405d2c2fa71c5ed90743a80242817bac359636a51b1f6f74273ce.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{OQ}$ is a chord of the circle\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{OR}$ is a radius of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{QR}$ is a diameter of the circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497398 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c258cfe8-9152-5d49-a8e8-a1c8668a4029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497398 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the endpoint $O$ does not lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{OQ}$ is a radius, but not a chord.\n\n- Statement II is true. The segment $\\overset{―}{OR}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overset{―}{OR}$ is a radius.\n\n- Statement III is false since the segment $\\overset{―}{QR}$ does not pass through the circle's center. Therefore, $\\overset{―}{QR}$ is a chord but not a diameter.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497412 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497412 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497412 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497412 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ec/ec14b1461a3125c2b4c320166230980ee33cbd004e07692e2c7763868f0edaa1.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{―}{OC}$ if $AB=15?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eefb1780-b1c4-5fe1-ae57-be7c2236e61c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497412 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r=OC.$ \n\nWe first notice that\n\n$$\nAB=AO+OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because $AB=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =15 \\\\ AO+OB & =15 \\\\ r+r & =15 \\\\ 2r & =15 \\\\ r & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nOC=r=7.5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
